Adding an H2 blocker (like ranitidine or famotidine) is often part of some protocols, as it can further reduce histamine-mediated gastric acid and some other reactions.

You could incorporate it in your prophylaxis “code” as follows:

```text
// Proposed prophylaxis timeline (example):
// 13 hours before imaging
IV methylprednisolone 40 mg
// 7 hours before imaging
IV methylprednisolone 40 mg
// 1 hour before imaging
IV methylprednisolone 40 mg + IV diphenhydramine 50 mg
// Also 1 hour before imaging
IV famotidine 20 mg (or ranitidine 50 mg)
```

Or place it on a separate line if you prefer clarity. The important point is that it’s given around the same time as your other medications, usually 1 hour before the procedure. Always confirm that your institution’s protocols or prescribing resources support adding H2-blockers, as practices may differ.

